    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Drawing</span> is one of the most fundamental skills in visual arts — the foundation upon which painting, design, and many other art forms are built. This topic examines still life and nature drawing, types of drawings, and shading techniques used to bring a drawing to life.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Drawing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Drawing</span> is the art of representing objects, scenes, or ideas on a flat surface using lines, tones, and shading, usually with tools such as pencils, charcoal, or ink. Good drawing begins with <strong>keen observation</strong> — carefully studying the shape, proportion, and details of the subject before putting pencil to paper.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Still Life and Nature Drawing</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Still life drawing</span> involves arranging and drawing inanimate objects (such as fruits, bottles, or household items), while <span class="learn-keyword">nature drawing</span> involves observing and drawing natural forms such as leaves, trees, animals, and landscapes. Both rely on close, patient observation of form, proportion, and light.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Types of Drawing and Surface Effects</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Outline drawing</td><td>Uses only lines/contours to define the shape of an object, without shading</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tonal drawing</td><td>Uses varying degrees of light and dark (shading) to give a sense of form and depth</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Gesture drawing</td><td>Quick, loose sketches capturing the overall movement or pose of a subject</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Detailed/rendered drawing</td><td>Careful, fully worked drawing showing fine detail, texture, and tone</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Shading Techniques</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>How It Works</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Cross-hatching</td><td>Overlapping sets of parallel lines drawn in different directions to build up tone</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Hatching (line shading)</td><td>Parallel lines drawn closely together; closer lines create darker tones</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Burnishing/burning</td><td>Rubbing or blending graphite/charcoal with a tool (or finger) to smooth and darken an area</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Pointillism (stippling)</td><td>Building up tone using small dots — more dots close together create darker areas</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wash</td><td>A thin, diluted layer of ink or watercolour applied to create smooth, graduated tone</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse cross-hatching (crossing lines) with hatching/line shading (parallel lines only) or pointillism (dots). Each shading technique produces a distinctly different surface effect.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Drawing represents objects using lines, tone, and shading, built on keen observation. Still life and nature drawing are key practice areas, and shading techniques such as cross-hatching, hatching, burnishing, pointillism, and wash all create different surface effects and tonal depth.\n</div>\n  ',
