    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Painting</span> takes drawing a step further, adding colour to create mood, depth, and expression. This lesson explores different painting styles, subjects, and how colour is mixed and applied.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Styles of Painting</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Style</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Animal hide painting</td><td>Painting done on prepared animal skins, common in some traditional African art</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mural painting</td><td>Large-scale painting done directly on walls or other permanent surfaces</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Impasto</td><td>Paint applied thickly, leaving visible brush or palette knife strokes for texture</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fresco</td><td>Painting done on freshly applied, wet plaster, so the pigment bonds with the wall as it dries</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tempera</td><td>A fast-drying paint made by mixing pigment with a binder, traditionally egg yolk</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Subjects for Painting</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Painters practice with a variety of subjects: <strong>still life objects</strong> (chairs, plates, kettles), <strong>natural objects</strong> (insects, animals, bones, flowers), and <strong>figure painting</strong> — which includes studying the parts of the human body, portraiture (the face and head), and full life painting (the entire human figure).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Colour Mixing and Application</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Primary colours</td><td>Red, yellow, and blue — cannot be created by mixing other colours</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Secondary colours</td><td>Formed by mixing two primary colours (e.g., red + yellow = orange)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tertiary colours</td><td>Formed by mixing a primary colour with a neighbouring secondary colour</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Tint</td><td>A colour lightened by adding white</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Shade</td><td>A colour darkened by adding black</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n<p class="learn-p">Colour can be applied using various tools (brushes, palette knives, sponges) and techniques (blending, layering, dry-brushing), each producing a different visual effect.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Figure Painting</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Figure painting</span> requires understanding human anatomy and proportion. <strong>Portrait painting</strong> focuses on the face and head, capturing likeness and expression, while <strong>full life painting</strong> depicts the entire human body, often studying its proportions, posture, and movement.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: Fresco is painted on WET plaster; Tempera uses egg yolk as a binder; Impasto is applied THICKLY for texture. These distinctions are commonly tested.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Painting styles include animal hide, mural, impasto, fresco, and tempera, each with distinct techniques. Painters practice with still life, natural objects, and figures (portrait and full life), guided by an understanding of colour mixing — primary, secondary, tertiary colours, tints, and shades.\n</div>\n  ',
