    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Festivals are rich sources of inspiration for visual artists, filled with symbolism, colour, costume, and body decoration. This lesson examines the meaning, types, and functions of Nigerian cultural festivals, and their artistic significance.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Origin of Festivals</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">festival</span> is a periodic celebration observed by a community, often rooted in religious beliefs, historical events, or seasonal changes such as harvests. Nigerian festivals typically originate from ancestral traditions, worship of deities, or the marking of significant agricultural or historical events.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Nigerian Festivals</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Festival</th><th>Type/Significance</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Eyo Festival</td><td>Lagos — masquerade festival honouring departed leaders</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Argungu Fishing Festival</td><td>Kebbi State — celebrates the fishing season with a fishing competition</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>New Yam Festival</td><td>Widely celebrated (e.g., Igbo areas) — marks the harvest of new yams</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Osun-Osogbo Festival</td><td>Osun State — religious festival honouring the river goddess Osun</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Durbar Festival</td><td>Northern Nigeria — celebrates Islamic holidays with horse-riding displays</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Functions of Festivals</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Festivals serve to preserve cultural heritage, strengthen community bonds, honour ancestors or deities, mark important agricultural cycles, and attract tourism. For visual artists, festivals also provide occasions for creating masks, costumes, body decorations, and other symbolic artworks.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Symbolic Significance and Body Decoration in Festivals</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Many festivals feature elaborate <strong>body decoration</strong> — using paint, cloth, beads, and other materials to transform participants into symbolic figures representing spirits, ancestors, or deities. These decorations often carry deep meaning, reflecting beliefs about power, protection, fertility, or status within the community.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn to match specific festivals to their states/regions and purposes — Eyo (Lagos, masquerade), Argungu (Kebbi, fishing), Osun-Osogbo (Osun, religious/river goddess).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigerian festivals are periodic community celebrations rooted in religion, history, or agriculture, serving functions such as preserving culture and honouring ancestors. They are rich in symbolic significance, especially through body decoration and costume — an important source of artistic material for visual artists.\n</div>\n  ',
