    topic: 'History of Art (African Traditional Art and Culture)',
    topicCode: 'SS2-VA-04',
    module: 'Art, Culture, and Tradition',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Before modern Nigerian art emerged, rich traditions of art flourished across West Africa. This lesson surveys traditional African art forms — their origins, materials, and lasting influence on modern art.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Traditional African Art Regions</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Region/Culture</th><th>Notable Characteristics</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Guinea</td><td>Known for wood carving traditions and ceremonial masks</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Kissi (Kiss)</td><td>Known for stone figures called "Kissi stones" (nomoli), used in ancestor veneration</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Cameroon (Grassfields)</td><td>Known for elaborate carved wooden thrones, masks, and beadwork</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Dahomey (present-day Benin Republic)</td><td>Known for appliqué textiles, bronze/brass casting, and royal art celebrating kings</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sierra Leone</td><td>Known for wood carving and mask-making traditions, including Sande society masks</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Media Used in Traditional African Art</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Traditional African artists worked with locally available materials, including <strong>wood</strong> (for carving masks, figures, and thrones), <strong>stone</strong> (for figures and monuments), <strong>bronze and brass</strong> (for casting royal and ceremonial objects using techniques like lost-wax casting), <strong>clay</strong> (for pottery and figures), and <strong>textiles</strong> (for appliqué and woven cloth).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Characteristics of Traditional African Art</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Traditional African art is often characterized by symbolic and stylized (rather than strictly realistic) representation, strong connections to religion and ancestor veneration, communal rather than purely individual purpose, and functional use in rituals, ceremonies, and everyday life rather than being created purely for display.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Influence on Modern Art</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Traditional African art has profoundly influenced modern art both within and outside Africa — inspiring stylization, abstraction, and symbolic forms seen in movements like Cubism (e.g., influencing artists such as Pablo Picasso), and continuing to inform the work of contemporary Nigerian and African artists who draw on ancestral techniques and motifs.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn to match each traditional art region to its notable art form: Guinea (wood carving/masks), Kissi (stone figures), Cameroon (carved thrones/beadwork), Dahomey (appliqué/bronze casting), Sierra Leone (masks/wood carving).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Traditional African art forms from regions like Guinea, Kissi, Cameroon, Dahomey, and Sierra Leone used materials such as wood, stone, bronze, and textiles to create symbolic, functional art tied to religion, ceremony, and community life — profoundly influencing both African and global modern art.\n</div>\n  ',
