    topic: 'Textile Design on Paper',
    topicCode: 'SS2-VA-06',
    module: 'Design and Applied Arts',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Before a pattern is woven or printed onto fabric, it is usually developed and tested on paper first. This lesson explores how designers create motifs and repeating patterns for textile design using paper as a planning medium.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Textile Design on Paper</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Textile design on paper</span> involves developing patterns and motifs on paper as a preparatory step before they are transferred or applied to actual fabric. It allows a designer to plan, test, and refine ideas cheaply and quickly before committing them to cloth.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Creating Motifs</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">motif</span> is a single decorative unit or design element — such as a shape, figure, or pattern — that forms the building block of a larger textile design. Motifs may be geometric (circles, triangles), figurative (animals, plants, human figures), or abstract.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Repeating Formats</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Repeat Format</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Block/straight repeat</td><td>Motif repeated in straight rows and columns, aligned directly beneath and beside each other</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Half-drop repeat</td><td>Alternating columns of motifs are offset (dropped) by half the motif\'s height</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Brick/half-brick repeat</td><td>Motifs offset horizontally by half their width, like bricks in a wall</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mirror repeat</td><td>Motif is reflected (mirrored) along an axis to create symmetry</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Diamond repeat</td><td>Motifs arranged in a diamond/diagonal grid pattern</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Application of Colour on Patterns</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Once a motif and repeat format have been established, colour is applied to enhance the design\'s visual impact. Designers consider colour harmony, contrast, and the eventual use of the fabric (e.g., clothing, curtains, upholstery) when choosing and applying colours to a textile pattern.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know the difference between a half-drop repeat (offset vertically) and a brick repeat (offset horizontally) — both create a staggered look but along different axes.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Textile design on paper involves creating motifs and testing repeating formats — such as block, half-drop, brick, mirror, and diamond repeats — before applying colour, allowing designers to plan patterns economically before committing them to fabric.\n</div>\n  ',
