    topic: 'Furniture Making',
    topicCode: 'SS2-VA-08',
    module: 'Design and Applied Arts',
    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Furniture combines functionality with artistry. This lesson examines the functions of furniture, the processes involved in making it, and how appropriate wood is sourced and treated before use.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Functions of Furniture</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Function</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Utility/practical use</td><td>Provides seating, storage, or surfaces for daily activities (chairs, tables, cupboards)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Comfort</td><td>Designed to support the body comfortably during use (e.g., cushioned seating)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Aesthetic value</td><td>Adds beauty and style to a room or space through its design, shape, and finish</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Status/symbolism</td><td>Certain furniture pieces (e.g., ornate thrones) can symbolize status or authority</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Processes Involved in Furniture Making</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Furniture making typically follows a sequence: <strong>design planning</strong> (sketching the intended piece), <strong>sourcing appropriate wood</strong>, <strong>measuring and cutting</strong> the wood to size, <strong>joining</strong> the pieces (using joints, nails, screws, or adhesives), <strong>shaping and smoothing</strong> (sanding), and <strong>finishing</strong> (varnishing, painting, or polishing).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Sourcing Appropriate Wood</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Selecting the right type of wood is essential for durable, quality furniture. Considerations include the wood\'s strength and hardness, resistance to pests (like termites), grain pattern and appearance, availability, and cost. Common woods used include mahogany, iroko, and teak, valued for their durability and appearance.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Wood Treatment Before Use</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Before wood is used in furniture making, it typically undergoes <strong>seasoning</strong> (drying out moisture content to prevent warping or cracking later), <strong>treatment against pests</strong> (such as termites and wood-boring insects), and sometimes <strong>preservative treatment</strong> to protect against decay and extend the wood\'s lifespan.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that seasoning wood (drying it to reduce moisture) is a crucial step BEFORE furniture making — using unseasoned (wet/green) wood can cause the finished furniture to warp or crack later.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Furniture serves functions including utility, comfort, aesthetics, and symbolism. Furniture making involves design planning, sourcing appropriate wood, cutting, joining, shaping, and finishing — with proper wood treatment (seasoning and pest protection) essential before construction begins.\n</div>\n  ',
