    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Puppetry</span> is a distinctive art form that combines sculpture, costume design, and performance, with deep roots in Nigerian and broader African cultural tradition. This lesson explores puppet types, construction, tools, and cultural significance.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Types of Puppets</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Marionette</td><td>A puppet controlled from above using strings attached to its limbs and body</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Hand/glove puppet</td><td>A puppet worn like a glove, controlled by the puppeteer\'s hand and fingers</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Rod puppet</td><td>A puppet controlled using rigid rods attached to its body and limbs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Shadow puppet</td><td>A flat puppet manipulated behind a lit screen, casting a shadow visible to the audience</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Tools and Materials for Puppet Construction</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Puppet-making requires materials such as wood, cloth, cardboard, wire, and stuffing, along with tools for cutting, carving, and sewing. Seams and stitches, similar to those used in embroidery, are important for constructing a puppet\'s costume and body covering.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Puppetry Construction Types</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Beyond the basic mechanical types, puppetry can be classified by its dramatic use: <strong>marionette theatre</strong> (string-controlled performance), <strong>storytelling puppetry</strong> (used to narrate folk tales and moral lessons), <strong>drama puppetry</strong> (used within staged plays), and <strong>shadow plays</strong> (performances using shadow puppets against a lit screen).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Nigerian Puppetry: Forms, Styles, and Cultural Significance</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nigerian puppetry traditions, such as those found among the Igbo (e.g., the Okumkpa and Ijele traditions) and other ethnic groups, have historically been used for storytelling, moral instruction, entertainment, and social commentary during festivals and ceremonies. Puppetry serves important cultural functions, preserving oral traditions and community values through performance.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn the mechanical distinctions: marionette (string-controlled from above), hand/glove puppet (worn on the hand), rod puppet (controlled by rigid rods), shadow puppet (flat, cast as a shadow).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Puppetry involves creating and manipulating figures such as marionettes, hand puppets, rod puppets, and shadow puppets, constructed using materials like wood, cloth, and wire. Nigerian puppetry traditions carry deep cultural significance, used for storytelling, moral instruction, and entertainment.\n</div>\n  ',
