    topic: 'Imaginative Composition I',
    topicCode: 'SS2-VA-10',
    module: 'Composition and Painting',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Not all art is copied directly from what the eye sees — much of it comes from the artist\'s imagination. This lesson introduces <span class="learn-keyword">imaginative composition</span>, exploring how artists arrange invented or remembered scenes into a coherent, well-balanced picture.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Imaginative Composition</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Imaginative composition</span> refers to the arrangement of subjects, objects, and scenes in a drawing or painting created from the artist\'s imagination, memory, or creative invention, rather than direct observation of a subject in front of them.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Components of a Composition</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Foreground</td><td>The part of the picture that appears closest to the viewer</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Middle ground</td><td>The area between the foreground and background, often where the main subject is placed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Background</td><td>The part of the picture that appears furthest from the viewer, often setting the scene</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Area/centre of interest</td><td>The focal point of the composition — the element that draws the viewer\'s eye first</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Compositional arrangement</td><td>How the various objects and elements are organized in relation to one another across the picture</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Principles Guiding Composition</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Good imaginative composition considers balance (distributing visual weight evenly), proportion (correct relative sizing of objects), unity (elements that work together harmoniously), and a clear centre of interest that guides the viewer\'s eye through the picture in a logical, pleasing way.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Practical Application in Drawing and Painting</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">When creating an imaginative composition, an artist first decides on a theme or subject, then plans the placement of the foreground, middle ground, and background elements, establishes the centre of interest, and finally develops the piece through drawing and/or painting techniques, applying appropriate shading and colour.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the order: foreground (closest), middle ground (where the main subject often sits), background (furthest, sets the scene). The area of interest is the focal point that grabs the viewer\'s attention.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Imaginative composition involves creating scenes from imagination or memory, organized using components such as foreground, middle ground, background, and a clear area of interest, applying principles of balance, proportion, and unity in the final drawing or painting.\n</div>\n  ',
