    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Building on the principles of imaginative composition, this lesson focuses on practical application — drawing everyday objects from memory and sketching or painting full scenes purely from imagination.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Drawing Objects from Imagination</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Artists practice drawing familiar objects — such as a pot, spoon, chair, stove, or house — entirely from memory and imagination, without a physical reference in front of them. This exercise strengthens an artist\'s visual memory and understanding of form, proportion, and structure.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Sketching and Painting Scenes from Memory</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Beyond single objects, artists also practice sketching and painting entire remembered or imagined scenes — such as a motor accident, a busy market scene, or a carpenter\'s workshop. These exercises require the artist to recall and creatively arrange multiple objects, figures, and their interactions within a believable composition.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Applying Colour and Shading Techniques</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Once a scene has been sketched, colour and shading techniques (as covered in earlier topics — hatching, cross-hatching, wash, etc.) are applied to bring the imaginative composition to life, adding depth, mood, and realism to the finished piece.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Benefits of Practicing Imaginative Composition</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Benefit</th><th>Explanation</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Strengthens visual memory</td><td>Trains the artist to recall shapes, proportions, and details without a live reference</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Builds creative confidence</td><td>Encourages original thinking and inventive problem-solving in composition</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Develops storytelling skills</td><td>Helps the artist convey narrative and mood through arrangement of scene elements</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Improves understanding of perspective</td><td>Requires the artist to logically place objects at appropriate depths within an imagined space</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Common exam scenes to practice imagining and describing include a market scene, a carpenter\'s workshop, and a motor accident scene — remember these as classic examples of imaginative composition practice.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Practical imaginative composition involves drawing everyday objects and sketching full scenes (like a market or workshop) from memory, then applying colour and shading to complete the piece. This practice strengthens visual memory, creativity, storytelling, and understanding of perspective.\n</div>\n  ',
