    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Nature has always been one of art\'s greatest sources of inspiration. This lesson explores <span class="learn-keyword">nature painting</span> — its meaning, subjects, components, and techniques of colour application.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning and Characteristics of Nature Painting</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Nature painting</span> involves creating artworks based on natural forms and subjects found in the environment. It is characterized by organic shapes, natural colour palettes, and careful attention to textures and forms found in the natural world.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Natural Objects Used as Subjects</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Common subjects for nature painting include <strong>plants</strong> (leaves, flowers, trees), <strong>animals</strong> (birds, insects, mammals), <strong>shells</strong>, <strong>skulls</strong> and bones, and <strong>mountains</strong> and other landscape features — each offering unique forms, textures, and colours for the artist to study and depict.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Arrangement and Components of Nature Painting</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">As with imaginative composition, nature painting requires thoughtful arrangement of foreground, middle ground, and background elements, along with a clear centre of interest, to create a balanced and visually engaging depiction of a natural subject or scene.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Techniques of Colour Application</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Impasto</td><td>Thick application of paint, often used to give texture to elements like tree bark or rocky surfaces</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fresco</td><td>Painting on wet plaster, historically used for large-scale nature and landscape murals</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wash/glazing</td><td>Thin, transparent layers of colour, useful for skies, water, and atmospheric effects</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Blending</td><td>Smoothly transitioning between colours to depict soft, gradual natural forms (e.g., clouds, petals)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Nature painting subjects (plants, animals, shells, skulls, mountains) are commonly tested — learn to distinguish "natural objects" from man-made or imaginative subjects covered in other topics.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nature painting depicts natural subjects such as plants, animals, shells, skulls, and mountains, using compositional arrangement principles similar to imaginative composition, and applying techniques like impasto, fresco, wash, and blending to capture natural textures and forms.\n</div>\n  ',
