    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Nigeria\'s visual arts have been shaped by both modern academic institutions and traditional art centres. This lesson surveys key art institutions, their courses and history, and their contributions to Nigerian art.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Modern Art Institutions in Nigeria</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Institution</th><th>Notable Contribution</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Obafemi Awolowo University (OAU), Ile-Ife</td><td>Renowned fine arts department, training generations of Nigerian artists</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Ahmadu Bello University (ABU), Zaria</td><td>Home of the influential "Zaria Art Society," which shaped modern Nigerian art philosophy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Adeyemi College of Education, Ondo</td><td>Trains art educators, contributing to art teaching across Nigeria</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Olabisi Onabanjo University</td><td>Offers fine and applied arts programmes contributing to Nigerian art education</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Traditional Art Institutions in Nigeria</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Centre</th><th>Notable Contribution</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Oshogbo Art Centre (Osun State)</td><td>Fostered the influential Oshogbo art movement, blending traditional and modern styles</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mbari Mbayo Art Centre</td><td>Provided a creative hub for artists, writers, and performers in Oshogbo during the 1960s</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nri Art Centre</td><td>Rooted in Igbo traditional art and bronze-casting heritage</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Courses Offered by Modern Art Institutions</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Modern Nigerian art institutions typically offer courses in painting, sculpture, graphic design, textile design, ceramics, and art education, combining practical studio work with theoretical art history and criticism.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Contributions to Nigerian Art</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Together, modern and traditional art institutions have shaped Nigerian art by training skilled artists and educators, preserving and evolving traditional techniques, fostering movements that blend indigenous and contemporary styles (such as the Oshogbo and Zaria art movements), and producing many of Nigeria\'s most celebrated artists.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the pairing: Ahmadu Bello University (Zaria) is associated with the influential Zaria Art Society, while Oshogbo Art Centre is associated with the Oshogbo art movement.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nigeria\'s art development has been shaped by modern institutions like OAU, ABU, Adeyemi College, and Olabisi Onabanjo University, alongside traditional centres like Oshogbo Art Centre, Mbari Mbayo, and Nri Art Centre — together training artists and blending indigenous with contemporary artistic styles.\n</div>\n  ',
