    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Pottery</span> is one of humanity\'s oldest crafts, transforming raw clay into functional and decorative objects. This lesson covers the meaning, tools, methods, functions, and marketing of pottery, as well as its distinction from ceramics.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Pottery</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Pottery</span> refers to the craft of shaping clay into objects — such as pots, bowls, and vases — and then hardening them, typically through firing in a kiln, to produce durable, functional, or decorative items.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Tools and Materials for Pottery</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Pottery-making requires materials such as clay, water, and sometimes glazes, along with tools including a <strong>potter\'s wheel</strong> (for shaping rounded forms), <strong>modelling tools</strong> (for shaping and detailing), a <strong>kiln</strong> (for firing/hardening the clay), and basic tools like sponges and ribs for smoothing surfaces.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Methods of Pottery Making</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Hand-building (pinch method)</td><td>Shaping clay by hand, pinching and pressing it into form without a wheel</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Coiling</td><td>Rolling clay into long coils, then stacking and joining them to build up a form</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Slab building</td><td>Rolling clay into flat slabs, then cutting and joining them to construct a form</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wheel throwing</td><td>Shaping clay on a rotating potter\'s wheel to create symmetrical, rounded forms</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Functions and Basic Terminologies of Pottery</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Pottery serves functions including storage (of water, grain, or oil), cooking, decoration, and ritual/ceremonial use. Basic terminology includes: <strong>greenware</strong> (unfired, still-wet or dry clay object), <strong>bisqueware</strong> (clay fired once, but not yet glazed), <strong>glaze</strong> (a liquid coating applied before a second firing to create a glassy, decorative, and waterproof surface), and <strong>kiln</strong> (the oven used to fire clay at high temperatures).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">5. Pottery vs Ceramics and Marketing Pottery Wares</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">While the terms are often used interchangeably, <span class="learn-keyword">pottery</span> typically refers specifically to clay-based objects, while <span class="learn-keyword">ceramics</span> is a broader term covering all objects made by shaping and firing inorganic materials (including pottery, but also things like tiles or industrial ceramics). Marketing pottery wares involves considering pricing, presentation, and appropriate sales outlets (such as markets, galleries, or craft fairs) to reach interested buyers.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: ALL pottery is ceramics, but NOT all ceramics is pottery (ceramics is the broader category, including tiles and industrial products beyond just clay pots).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Pottery is the craft of shaping and firing clay into functional or decorative objects, using methods like hand-building, coiling, slab building, and wheel throwing, with tools such as a potter\'s wheel and kiln. Pottery is a subset of the broader category of ceramics.\n</div>\n  ',
