    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Building on the earlier introduction to graphic design, this lesson takes a closer, more detailed look specifically at book cover and jacket design — their features, differences, and practical creation.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Book Cover and Book Jacket</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">book cover</span> is the front (and back) protective and decorative covering directly attached to a book, while a <span class="learn-keyword">book jacket</span> is a separate, removable printed paper wrap that fits around a hardcover book, often providing additional promotional information and visual appeal.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Differences Between Book Cover and Book Jacket</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Book Cover</th><th>Book Jacket</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Permanently attached to the book</td><td>Removable, wraps around the book</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Common on paperback books</td><td>Common on hardcover books</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Simpler in structure</td><td>Often includes folded flaps with additional information</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Features of Book Design</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Spine</td><td>The bound edge of the book, visible when shelved, usually showing the title and author</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Blurb</td><td>A short promotional description of the book\'s content, usually on the back cover</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Flap</td><td>The folded inner section of a book jacket, often containing the blurb or author bio</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>ISBN</td><td>International Standard Book Number — a unique identifying code for the book, often with a barcode</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Author bio</td><td>A short biography of the book\'s author, often with a photo</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Book summary</td><td>A brief overview of the book\'s content or storyline</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Publisher\'s address</td><td>Information identifying the publishing company and its location</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Practical Design of Book Covers and Jackets</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Practical book cover and jacket design involves sketching the layout in pencil, arranging text (title, author name) and imagery in a balanced composition, and then applying colour to complete a visually appealing and marketable design that reflects the book\'s genre and content.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember: the blurb describes the BOOK\'s content; the author bio describes the AUTHOR. Both may appear on a cover or jacket, but they serve different purposes.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> A book cover is attached directly to a book, while a book jacket is a removable wrap, often used on hardcovers. Both feature elements like the spine, blurb, flap, ISBN, author bio, book summary, and publisher\'s address, all combined through practical pencil and colour design work.\n</div>\n  ',
