    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  All complex designs begin with simple building blocks. This lesson introduces basic and geometrical shapes, and how they can be arranged harmoniously to create effective designs.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Basic Shapes and Design</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Basic shapes</span> are simple, fundamental forms — such as circles, squares, and triangles — that serve as the building blocks for more complex designs. A <span class="learn-keyword">design</span> is the deliberate, planned arrangement of these shapes (and other elements) to create a visually pleasing or functional composition.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Basic and Geometrical Shapes</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Shape</th><th>Characteristics</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Circle</td><td>A round shape with no corners, all points equidistant from the centre</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Square</td><td>A four-sided shape with equal sides and four right angles</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Triangle</td><td>A three-sided shape with three angles</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Rectangle</td><td>A four-sided shape with opposite sides equal and four right angles</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Non-basic/organic shapes</td><td>Irregular, free-form shapes not based on strict geometric rules (e.g., leaf shapes, blob shapes)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Materials and Tools for Basic Design</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Basic design work typically uses pencils, rulers, set squares, compasses (for circles), and colour media such as crayons or paint, applied on paper or board.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Practical Approach and Arrangement of Shapes</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A practical approach to basic design begins with sketching accurate shapes in pencil, then applying colour thoughtfully. Arranging both basic (geometric) and non-basic (organic) shapes in a harmonious design requires attention to balance, repetition, contrast, and proportion, ensuring the overall composition feels unified and visually pleasing.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the key distinguishing features: circle (no corners, equidistant points from centre), square (equal sides, right angles), triangle (three sides), rectangle (opposite sides equal, right angles).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Basic shapes — circles, squares, triangles, and rectangles — along with non-basic organic shapes, form the building blocks of design. Using tools like pencils, rulers, and compasses, these shapes can be arranged harmoniously through principles like balance and proportion to create effective designs.\n</div>\n  ',
