    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Nigerian indigenous art is rich with decorative motifs — recurring visual elements passed down through generations. This lesson examines the meaning of motifs, common indigenous themes, and formats for arranging them into patterns.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Motifs</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A <span class="learn-keyword">motif</span> is a recurring decorative element or design unit, often carrying symbolic or cultural meaning, that is repeated to create a larger pattern in indigenous art forms such as textiles, pottery, and body decoration.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Indigenous Decorative Motifs</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Motif Category</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Floral motifs</td><td>Flowers, leaves, and plant-based decorative patterns</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Animal motifs</td><td>Birds, snakes, and other animal forms with symbolic meaning</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Object motifs</td><td>Everyday or ceremonial objects (e.g., pots, tools) stylized into decorative patterns</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Formats for Motif Making</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Half-drop</td><td>Alternating columns of motifs offset vertically by half the motif\'s height</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Diamond</td><td>Motifs arranged along a diagonal, diamond-shaped grid</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Mirror</td><td>Motifs reflected along an axis to create symmetry</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Simple repeat</td><td>The same motif repeated in a straightforward, evenly spaced pattern</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Creating Patterns Using Indigenous Motifs</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Creating a pattern using indigenous motifs involves selecting a culturally meaningful motif, choosing an appropriate arrangement format, and applying it consistently across a surface — whether fabric, pottery, or another medium — while considering colour, scale, and spacing to produce a cohesive, visually appealing design.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> These motif format terms (half-drop, diamond, mirror, simple repeat) overlap with the "Textile Design on Paper" topic — review both together, as they are frequently tested in combination.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Motifs are recurring, culturally meaningful decorative elements, including floral, animal, and object types in Nigerian indigenous art. They are arranged using formats such as half-drop, diamond, mirror, and simple repeat to create cohesive, visually appealing patterns.\n</div>\n  ',
