    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Building on the introduction to calabash decoration, this lesson focuses on the practical tools, preparation steps, and design planning needed to create a well-decorated calabash piece.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Tools and Materials for Calabash Decoration</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Tool/Material</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Carving/engraving knife</td><td>Used to cut or scratch designs into the calabash surface</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Heated metal tool</td><td>Used for burning (pyro-engraving) patterns into the surface</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Paint and brushes</td><td>Used for applying painted decoration and colour</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Beads and adhesive</td><td>Used for attaching beaded decoration to the surface</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sandpaper</td><td>Used to smooth the calabash surface before decoration begins</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Preparing the Calabash for Decoration</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Before decoration begins, the calabash must be properly dried and hollowed out, cleaned of any remaining pulp or seeds, and its surface smoothed (often using sandpaper) to provide a suitable, even surface for painting, engraving, or beading.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Planning the Decorative Design</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">As with other decorative art forms, planning a calabash design typically involves selecting an appropriate motif or pattern (often reflecting local cultural symbolism), sketching the design lightly onto the surface first, and considering how the chosen decoration method (painting, engraving, beading, or burning) will best express that pattern given the calabash\'s curved surface.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Practical Steps in Creating a Decorated Calabash</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The practical process typically follows this order: preparing and smoothing the surface, sketching the design lightly, applying the chosen decoration method (carving, burning, painting, or beading), and finally, if needed, applying a protective finish or sealant to preserve the decorated surface.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that surface preparation (cleaning, drying, smoothing) always comes BEFORE any decoration is applied — this preparatory step is essential and frequently tested.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Calabash decoration requires specific tools such as carving knives, heated tools, paint, and beads, alongside careful surface preparation (cleaning, drying, smoothing) before decoration begins. The practical process involves planning the design, sketching lightly, applying the chosen decoration method, and finishing with a protective sealant if needed.\n</div>\n  ',
