    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Body decoration is among the oldest and most personal forms of visual art. This lesson explores indigenous Nigerian traditions of body marking and decoration, including tattoo-like scarification and temporary body art.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Indigenous Body Decoration</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Indigenous body decoration</span> refers to traditional practices of marking or adorning the human body using motifs and patterns that carry cultural, social, or spiritual significance. This includes both permanent methods (such as scarification, akin to tattooing) and temporary methods (such as painting with dyes like henna or camwood).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Types of Indigenous Body Decoration</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Scarification</td><td>Permanent markings created by cutting or scarring the skin in patterned designs</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Henna/dye body art</td><td>Temporary designs painted onto the skin using natural dyes such as henna</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Body painting</td><td>Temporary designs applied using coloured pigments, often for festivals or ceremonies</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Symbolic Meaning of Body Decoration Motifs</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Motifs used in indigenous body decoration often carry deep symbolic meaning, indicating a person\'s ethnic identity, social status, age grade, marital status, spiritual protection, or rite of passage within their community. Patterns may be geometric, floral, or based on culturally significant symbols specific to an ethnic group.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Cultural Significance and Modern Context</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Traditionally, body decoration served important social functions — marking important life stages, community belonging, and beauty ideals. In contemporary times, some traditional practices have declined, while others (like henna art) remain popular for ceremonial and decorative purposes, and modern tattooing has also become a distinct, related art form influenced by both global and indigenous traditions.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Distinguish scarification (permanent, cut into skin) from henna/body painting (temporary, applied with dye or pigment on top of the skin) — a commonly tested distinction.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Indigenous body decoration includes permanent methods like scarification and temporary methods like henna and body painting, with motifs carrying symbolic meanings related to identity, status, and life stages. These traditions reflect deep cultural significance while continuing to evolve in modern contexts.\n</div>\n  ',
