    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Beyond the human body, indigenous Nigerian artists have long decorated the walls of homes and shrines with symbolic mural designs. This lesson explores traditional Nigerian mural art, its motifs, materials, and cultural functions.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Meaning of Indigenous Mural Design</h3>\n<p class="learn-p"><span class="learn-keyword">Mural design</span> refers to artwork created directly on walls, typically using motifs and patterns with cultural or symbolic significance. In indigenous Nigerian contexts, murals have traditionally decorated the exterior and interior walls of homes, shrines, and community buildings.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Traditional Nigerian Mural Traditions</h3>\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Tradition</th><th>Notable Characteristics</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Uli mural art (Igbo)</td><td>Flowing, linear, often abstract designs traditionally painted by women on walls and bodies</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nsukka/Igbo house painting</td><td>Wall decorations using natural pigments to depict symbolic and geometric patterns</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Hausa/Fulani wall decoration</td><td>Geometric and relief patterns often found decorating the exterior walls of traditional homes</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Materials Used in Traditional Mural Art</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Traditional mural artists used naturally available materials, including <strong>natural pigments</strong> (derived from plants, minerals, and charcoal), <strong>mud/clay</strong> (for relief and textured wall designs), and simple tools such as sticks, brushes made from plant fibres, or fingers, to apply designs directly onto prepared wall surfaces.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Symbolic and Cultural Functions of Murals</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Indigenous murals served functions including expressing spiritual or religious beliefs (particularly on shrine walls), marking social status or the significance of a household, celebrating important events, and preserving cultural stories and symbols passed down through generations of artists, often women in many traditions.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Uli art (Igbo) is a frequently tested traditional Nigerian mural/body art form — remember it is characterized by flowing, linear, often abstract designs, traditionally created by women.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Indigenous Nigerian mural design decorates walls of homes, shrines, and community buildings with culturally symbolic motifs, as seen in traditions like Uli (Igbo) and Hausa/Fulani wall decoration, using natural materials such as pigments and mud, and serving spiritual, social, and cultural functions.\n</div>\n  ',
